    "methodology": "10,000-game solitaire heuristic. Sequencing: land drop, cheapest ramp, commander on affordability, combo/assembly pieces, biggest affordable threat, then other proactive spells. Mana is spent once per turn: the turn's pool is built after the land drop and each cast removes the sources it paid with, so the whole turn's casts (plus the combo and engine-activation gates, which are checked against what is left) never exceed the available mana (DEC-937). Sources that come online untapped mid-turn join the pool. Cast priority holds mana for the line instead of emptying the pool into filler (DEC-944). The commander, mana sources and the cards named by the deck's combo gate or KEY_COMBOS assembly slots are cast freely \u2014 mana sources are mana-positive across the turn and a piece cast advances a line rather than spending it. Every other cast is filler, and filler is skipped when the pool it would leave behind no longer pays for (a) a combo or assembly line that was reachable this turn before the cast, or (b) the deck's configured paid activations: one commander Egg activation while the hatch engine is online (DEC-938) and one Aggravated Assault combat while the enchantment is out with an attacker (DEC-940). The gate outranks the activations: an Egg activation, a Skullclamp equip or an extra combat is itself skipped when paying for it would lose a reachable gate. A hold the pool cannot afford at all is dropped rather than left blocking development. One activation of each kind is held, not an open-ended budget. Decks with no combo win condition, no KEY_COMBOS entry and no configured engine have no holds and sequence exactly as before. Meaningful board = pressure score >= 8 or 2+ threats. Lethal proxy = cumulative combat-pressure proxy >= 40; combat pressure uses mana-value-based creature scoring (min(8, MV) plus one for a listed combat keyword) so the curve stays comparable across the fleet \u2014 printed power/toughness is in the card metadata since DEC-940 but is deliberately not used for the damage sum, only for the combat modifiers' power gates. Combo lethal = at least one winConditions[] entry tagged kind:'combo' has all pieces present (hand, battlefield, or substitutable) and combined mana cost payable. Substitution priority for missing pieces: (1) bargain free-cast tutor (e.g. Beseech the Mirror) when a non-rock enchantment is on battlefield OR commander has been in play for 1+ turn (approximates Sauron-style amass token from real-game opponent casts); (2) type-matched regular tutor whose tutor_targets includes piece's card type and tutor_mv_cap permits piece's MV; (3) reanimation spell (decklist[].primary_role/secondary_role == 'recursion') for permanent pieces only \u2014 cost replaces piece's cast cost (treats piece as if in graveyard). Each substitute is consumed once per combo line. Tutors, non-permanent combo pieces, and reanimation spells are reserved from filler casting. v2 limitations: no literal graveyard tracking (reanim assumes piece could be in GY), no opponent interaction, no multi-turn assembly tracking, no stack-trigger ordering. Commander hatch engine modeled (DEC-938): with the commander on the battlefield and at least one instant-speed sacrifice outlet (3 free/unlimited, 1 once-per-untap) online, each Egg that dies reveals from the top of the library until a creature card and puts it onto the battlefield free \u2014 a put, not a cast, so it credits impact, combat and draw like a cast but never re-triggers a cast-gated model. Egg supply per own turn: commander activations paid at 2 generic out of what the turn's casts left, one per untap and at most 4 before doublers (Thornbite Staff untaps her per death, but only while Ashnod's Altar is out to pay the next activation and only if the Staff was on the battlefield when she entered), plus entry Eggs (Palani's Hatcher's two, doubled again by Elesh Norn) and one per land drop from a landfall Egg maker the turn after it lands. Each of 2 token doublers doubles every Egg; each of 2 trigger doublers adds one hatch per death. Skullclamp equips own-turn Eggs at 1 generic each and credits 2 cards, but only the 0/1 Eggs \u2014 its +1/-1 leaves Nesting Dragon's 0/2 Dragon Egg alive as a 1/1, so those are tracked apart and only the free/limited outlets eat them. Seedborn Muse adds 3 free activations on the opponents' untap steps (instant-speed outlets only; those creatures are ready next turn regardless of haste). Attack-triggered hatches (Raph & Mikey) enter attacking and are ready the same combat. Hard cap 8 hatches per turn \u2014 the Thornbite infinite is scored by the combo systems, not here. Floors: no blockers or opponents, Eggs left over never block or trade, the Dinosaur and Dragon tokens that Egg sacrifices leave behind are not credited, Skullclamp and Palani's Hatcher do not open the engine on their own, ramp-fetched lands do not trigger landfall, and Maskwood Nexus turning every creature into an Egg, the paid Zopandrel/Mondrak outlets and opponent-end-step hatching beyond Seedborn are unmodelled. Team-haste rule modeled (DEC-938): the combat profile ignores printed Haste fleet-wide, so a creature that enters \u2014 cast or hatched \u2014 while any of 2 continuous haste grants, both halves of 1 configured pair, or 1 entry-gated grant (riot) is on the battlefield is combat-ready the turn it entered, as are 1 printed-haste creatures. The commander's tap ability reads the continuous grants live (they cover her retroactively) but reads riot only off her own entry record, since riot never hastes a creature already on the battlefield. Floor: the entry-time rule misses other creatures already out when a continuous source lands \u2014 at most one creature-turn each. Combat modifiers modeled (DEC-940, DEC-935 Phase 2): the base combat proxy is unchanged \u2014 every creature still scores min(8, MV) plus one for a listed keyword, so this deck's per-creature values stay comparable with the rest of the fleet, and printed power is read only by the power gate below. What changes is that the turn can hold up to 4 combat phases, and each phase applies, in this order: Zopandrel's beginning-of-combat team doubling (until end of turn, so it compounds once per phase a creature was present for), Xenagos doubling the single largest other attacker that was already on the battlefield when the trigger went on the stack, then Raphael doubling the whole phase (double strike). Extra phases come from Aurelia and Tifa (first combat only; Tifa gated on a ready attacker with printed power >= 7; Annie Joins Up doubles both legendary triggers), Combat Celebrant's exert (it untaps all other creatures, so it sits out the rest of the turn's combats and does not untap next turn), and Aggravated Assault paid at 3 generic + 2 red out of whatever the turn's casts and Egg activations left, repeating while the pool allows. The per-combat hatch triggers (Palani's Hatcher's Egg sacrifice, Raph & Mikey's attack hatch) fire again at each extra combat, and a creature that enters during a combat phase counts in that phase but is not a legal Xenagos target until the next one. Floors, not ceilings: no blockers, no opponents and no fogs, so every phase connects; Annie also doubling Zopandrel's trigger (x4 per phase), Roaming Throne under Maskwood Nexus doubling the combat triggers again, Ghalta and Mavren's attack token, and Combat Celebrant untapping off a later untap-all effect are all unmodelled. Only this deck is configured; shield-doctrine, armory-protocol, hidden-legion, gathering-shadow, anvil-chorus and eternal-agony carry comparable effects and can opt in later, each with its own review and a calibration re-run."
